	abstract = {An unknown prior density \$g({\textbackslash}theta )\$ has yielded realizations \${\textbackslash}Theta \_1,{\textbackslash}ldots ,{\textbackslash}Theta \_N\$. They are unobservable, but each \${\textbackslash}Theta \_i\$ produces an observable value \$X\_i\$ according to a known probability mechanism, such as \$X\_i{\textbackslash}sim \{{\textbackslash}rm Po\}({\textbackslash}Theta \_i)\$. We wish to estimate \$g({\textbackslash}theta )\$ from the observed sample \$X\_1,{\textbackslash}ldots ,X\_N\$. Traditional asymptotic calculations are discouraging, indicating very slow nonparametric rates of convergence. In this article we show that parametric exponential family modelling of \$g({\textbackslash}theta )\$ can give useful estimates in moderate-sized samples. We illustrate the approach with a variety of real and artificial examples. Covariate information can be incorporated into the deconvolution process, leading to a more detailed theory of generalized linear mixed models.},
