	abstract = {Let \${\textbackslash}mathbf\{S\}\_\{p {\textbackslash}times p\}\$ have a Wishart distribution with scale matrix \${\textbackslash}Sigma\$ and \$k\$ degrees of freedom. Estimators of \${\textbackslash}Sigma\$ are given for each of the loss functions \$L\_1({\textbackslash}hat\{{\textbackslash}Sigma\}, {\textbackslash}Sigma) = {\textbackslash}operatorname\{tr\} ({\textbackslash}hat\{{\textbackslash}Sigma\}{\textbackslash}Sigma{\textasciicircum}\{-1\}) - {\textbackslash}log {\textbackslash}det ({\textbackslash}hat\{{\textbackslash}Sigma\}{\textbackslash}Sigma{\textasciicircum}\{-1\}) - p\$ and \$L\_2({\textbackslash}hat\{{\textbackslash}Sigma\}, {\textbackslash}Sigma) = {\textbackslash}operatorname\{tr\} ({\textbackslash}hat\{{\textbackslash}Sigma\}{\textbackslash}Sigma{\textasciicircum}\{-1\} - I){\textasciicircum}2\$. The obvious estimators of \${\textbackslash}Sigma\$ are the scalar multiples of \${\textbackslash}mathbf\{S\}\$, i.e., \$a{\textbackslash}mathbf\{S\}\$ where \$0 {\textless} a {\textbackslash}leqslant 1/k\$. (Recall that \$(1/k){\textbackslash}mathbf\{S\}\$ is unbiased.) For each problem \$({\textbackslash}Sigma, {\textbackslash}hat\{{\textbackslash}Sigma\}, L\_i), i = 1, 2\$, we provide empirical Bayes estimators which dominate \$a{\textbackslash}mathbf\{S\}\$ by a substantial amount. It is seen that the uniform reduction in the risk function determined by \$L\_2\$ is at least \$100(p + 1)/(k + p + 1)\{{\textbackslash}tt{\textbackslash}\%\}\$. Dominance results for \$L\_1\$ and \$L\_2\$ were first given by James and Stein.},
	number = {3},
